y is jewish my mom's  family is german  so i have like germans jews former  soviets  you know there's on one hand the german  intellectual tradition is full of  brilliant thinkers who are also you know  kind of like anti-semitic or not very  favorably disposed toward the jews and  the jewish tradition has many brilliant  thinkers who maybe aren't favorably  predisposed to other figures and then  there's the russian and i was born in  canada so there's like a sort of  politeness that allows me to be with all  of these different positions in the  first place  and my training you know so all of these  factors and influences together they  just produced this result i guess that i  don't  find it necessary  to shut down a thinker when you can see  that there's something there like i'm  not saying that you have to go out and  you know hug a nazi or something like  that we're not just talking about  anybody with a controversial opinion  here there are all kinds of knuckleheads  and much worse  but  what do you do when it comes to someone  who's obviously brilliant  what do you do when it comes to someone  you can clearly learn from  you know we all know the proverb of  throwing out the baby with the bathwater  so you have to decide in each case do  you want to do that or you know do you  want to raise the baby and see what  comes of it so to speak  you just learn with enough experience  that there are people who would like you  to throw out a thinker entirely  not because that thinker was a nazi  but because in studying them you'll  learn something that's dangerous  for these people's control over your  mind  they want to control what you think they  want to control how you feel and they  don't want to give you any outlet  any escape  and so it's very convenient to raise  this guilt by association charge against  people oh the only reason you're reading  heidegger is because he was a nazi you  must be one the only reason you're  reading schmidt is because you're a  neo-nazi or a semi-fascist  that's bogus that's manipulation that's  gaslighting in a big way so  separating it out  helps us to be free  now i know  people listening to this care about  their freedom well if everything is  getting collapsed you know the roof is  falling in on us and everywhere these  accusations of guilt by association and  fascist and nazi are affecting our  ability to move  we're just trapped under the rubble  there's no freedom in that we need to  separate things out we need to make  these distinctions and just find our  point of orientation so in that sense  it's a little bit of the autobiography  it's a little bit of the experience it's  i see i think it's seeing through the  way that these accusations are being  used to blind us  and to control us  and  you know it's philosophy philosophy  teaches you that you just have to be  patient with great ideas  doesn't necessarily mean you follow them  everywhere they lead but you don't even  know where they're going to lead until  you start looking at them carefully  i thought that we could wrap up with a  couple of rapid fire questions that i'd  be fascinated to hear uh your  perspective on or your answers  the first one is when you talk to  individuals are there one or two  questions that you ask them that help  you get at how they think what their  world view is or who they are as a  person  usually if i'm working with a student  who's just come to me i'll ask them  what's  what are you thinking about right now  like what thoughts are on your mind  right what questions are concerning you  what have you been reading lately  because  everybody has something present to them  you know they're worried about i have  one student who's really worried about  where things are going in education  somebody else is really worried about  masculinity the attack on masculinity  traditional masculinity what does it  mean to be a man  somebody else their primary concern is  with how do i be a believer how can i be  a believer today in a world that seems  to be attacking faith  so i like to ask people you know what  what's there for you now what are your  concerns and then that's where we start  what about your favorite book if you had  to answer the question you know i want  to learn more about philosophy where  should i start what would that answer be  so  the two major philosophers for me are  plato and heidegger they're difficult to  approach  if you're just starting but i would  recommend plato's republic or something  by plato just to get your feet wet even  if you don't understand it even if you  feel lost it could be  plato's apology which is where socrates  is speaking before the athenians in  defense of his life after they have  found him guilty of corrupting the youth  and sentenced them to death  plato and heidegger the beginning and  end of philosophy those are my sweet  spots although like i say you're not  gonna get it at first glance so here's  where i mention  all the credit  everything i've ever learned in  philosophy i want to give like 99 of the  credit to this guy named leo strauss so  one of the best ways to get an  introduction to the history of political  philosophy is by reading leo strauss  he's the one who taught a generation of  students that you should read the whole  book carefully he's the one who taught a  generation of students not to rely on  bad translations and he's the one who  showed that it's possible to think about  the old questions they're not irrelevant  anymore we don't just learn about plato  we learn from plato all of those kinds  of insights i owe to strauss and so  that's what i would recommend plato  heidegger and leo strauss  when  you think about your daily schedule  somebody who studies philosophy someone  who is familiar with the stoics uh  people may think that you just sit in  the dark all day and just think to  yourself and you're meditating and so  calm what does your daily schedule look  like in in reality  it doesn't look like that i have three  kids okay i have two boys and a girl i  work from home my kids just are uh in  the other room right now home from lunch  so it's always hectic it's always crazy  married 10 years uh in two days  so life is full it's very full it's not  empty i'm not on a mountain i'm not all  by myself and i'm not in the desert okay  uh  students business business partners  family friends kids it's everything it's  a full life  but you just learn to navigate that life  in a different sort of way  i read in the evenings i read in the  mornings i read the day i made my work  obviously my study as well so when i'm  doing work tutoring or teaching or  recording i'm also that's just lucky in  my case not everybody's going to be able  to do that  take your desire to read philosophy and  merge it with your work  but but that's what it looks like it  looks like a full life  family friends kids the whole shebang  and finding some time to read to write  to take classes to listen to people and  to uh think what about diet and sleep do  you do anything special there  no i don't think i would be a good role  model here in any way i sometimes i  sleep too much sometimes too little i  was a vegan for a long time now i'm not  so sure that's particularly healthy so  i have no advice about that everybody's  got to find what works for them i'm  surprised anybody could just eat steak  all day like jordan peterson does but i  guess that's good for some people  um  what can i say yeah i wouldn't take  myself as a role model here in any case  we got to get you a 8-sleep mattress  we've got these these mattresses uh a  company that i invested in and as they  sponsor the podcast they uh have like a  thermoregulated uh mattress so you can  make it super cold and uh hence the name  eight sleep get eight hours it's uh the  one thing i'm religious about now yeah  um my last question for you is kind of a  fun one do you believe in aliens you  think that there's life  off of earth  and is there anything in history or via  the study of philosophy that would  suggest uh kind of non-human life uh  that that we should consider  there's nothing in the philosophers that  i study and that i teach  on this topic although in my opinion  that shouldn't stop us from thinking  about it because if we're trying to  consider the meaning of human history  human life and the cosmos obviously  aliens are a relevant consideration so  except for that which is that i think  they have a place in our thinking i  don't really have a position on whether  there's evidence whether they do or  don't exist  i only think that it would be a relevant  piece of information you know because  let's say somebody argues that human  nature is fixed and then when humans and  aliens first mix or meat  some sort of weird transformation  happens by osmosis well okay that kind  of thing would have to be taken into  consideration but otherwise i'm agnostic  about i don't know enough about it i  used to be pretty sure well okay who's  this guy his name is like uh steven  drear or something like that  anyways  aliens probably do exist but i don't  really know the details yeah it is um uh  it is one of the questions that  fascinates me uh because if we  discovered aliens tomorrow it would be  absolutely mind-bending for billions of  people here on earth and it would have  an impact on so many different things  yet  when you think about the importance of  it  so few people actually care to think or  investigate it and so there's just this  dichotomy between uh wow that would be  pretty crazy uh but nobody really  cares too much so i don't know i just  find it very fascinating  yeah absolutely i mean thinking through  what would it be like what if they were  this what if they were that right what  would we have to change in our own  self-understanding if there were aliens  that's not my primary area but for sure  there's a lot to think about there and  in general you know there's so much  worth thinking about um  we need all the help we can get from the  old books up to date with our new  situation our new science our new  technology our new insights it's  brilliant you'll never be bored it's a  it's an amazing world and it's uh it's  there for us to consider  michael where can we send people to find  you on the internet or find out more  about the school or any of the books  that you've written  so the shortest link is  duganbook.com you'll see my two books my  school my newsletter my social media and  all of that i have a lot of free videos  on youtube on political philosophy and  that's where i would go duganbook.com  that'll take you everywhere else you  need to go  awesome well michael thank you so much  for your time today i've learned a ton  uh and i've really enjoyed looking into  not only the work that you've done  but but also just kind of your world  view and understanding how you think  about  investigating these individuals your  ability to separate a number of their  different ideas and really the  commendable ability that you have to  hold various ideas that may conflict  with each other kind of in your head at  once and not necessarily go in either  direction but just contemplate you know  where have we been what have people in  the past thought about and spoken about  and then what is the impact on today's  society and what does that mean for  where we're going it's it's a uh  a task that i don't think ever ends uh  but watching you do it and kind of  learning more about your work has been  pretty cool for me so i appreciate  taking the time to talk today  thanks it's been my pleasure
 

